WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.650
Many adults derive their meaning

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.200
in life from having
children, having families.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.620
I don't have children.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.753
So it's been very important to
find or even impose

00:00:09.753 --> 00:00:11.300
meaning on my life.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.710
I call the MDMA, the
chemical of connection.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.280
This medicine encourages you to reach

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.246
out and figuratively or
literally touch another person

00:00:20.246 --> 00:00:22.650
and deepen that connection.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.794
And that's been very meaningful to me.

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.937
Better things for better living

00:00:26.937 --> 00:00:28.590
Through chemistry for...

00:00:28.842 --> 00:00:31.557
Better living through chemistry
was the official slogan

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.800
of DuPont chemicals for decades.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.150
It became the ironic cri de
coeur of counterculture hippies

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.760
in the 1960s, who started to experiment

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.710
with psychedelic drugs,
ranging from LSD to Mescalin

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.930
to magic mushrooms.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.480
One of those old hippies
is Charley Wininger,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.461
a 71 year old Brooklyn-based
psychotherapist

00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:52.340
and relationship specialist

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.426
who New York Newsday once
dubbed "The Love Doctor"

00:00:55.426 --> 00:00:57.060
Wininger has recently come out

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.340
of what he calls "the chemical closet"

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.110
with his new book 'Listening to Ecstasy:

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.760
The Transformative Power of MDMA'

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.580
which is both a how-to
guide and memoir of how

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.682
this drug impacted his relationship

00:01:09.682 --> 00:01:12.520
with his wife, Shelley, a retired nurse

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.060
In 2000, she was out

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.450
of a bad marriage, a repressive marriage

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.530
and she wanted to spread
her wings and fly.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.410
And she learned that I was a psychonaut,

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.780
one who does psychedelics.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.320
And this book is largely about our story

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.070
and how MDMA can really be used

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.740
as emotional super glue for relationships

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.650
and added a whole other layer

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.540
of intimacy and closeness for us.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.980
Wininger's on the forefront

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.990
of a psychedelic Renaissance,
that consciously builds

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.360
on the lessons of the
1960s to deliver not just

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.190
more effective therapy, but
what he calls "serious fun."

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.979
Fritz Perls famously said,
lose your mind and come

00:01:54.979 --> 00:01:56.620
to your senses.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.480
When I'm high on MDMA,
my mind quiets down,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:06.370
And I feel my heart and I
feel this central aliveness,

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.670
this, this chemical
affirmation of my spirit.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.340
And it's like, the sun
itself is rising in my heart.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.010
It's like taking a chemical
helicopter ride above my life.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:23.740
And I could look out at
where I've been, where I am

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.998
in my life and perhaps to
the horizon where I wanna go.

00:02:27.998 --> 00:02:29.880
And then I can come back down

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.860
and rededicate myself to the
way that I want to be living.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.910
MDMA was first synthesized
by pharmaceutical giant,

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.560
Merck back in 1912 and
rediscovered decades later

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.610
in the mid 1960s by Maverick
chemist, Alexander Shulgin

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.820
who liked its ability to
increase emotions and empathy

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:48.830
among users.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.180
Therapists started using it

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.779
in counseling sessions, but
after it gained a reputation

00:02:52.779 --> 00:02:54.740
as a party and rave drug,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.057
it was banned by the Drug
Enforcement Administration

00:02:57.057 --> 00:02:59.070
in 1985.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.970
Thanks to the decades long efforts

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.160
of the Multidisciplinary Association

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.340
for Psychedelic Studies or M.A.P.S.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.809
MDMA assisted psychotherapy is now

00:03:07.809 --> 00:03:11.990
at stage three clinical
trials for FDA approval.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:14.546
If the experience of
marijuana is any indication,

00:03:14.546 --> 00:03:18.150
medical use could eventually
lead to the legalization

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.680
of MDMA for recreational
purposes, as well.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.540
As befits both a tribal elder
and a medical professional,

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.730
Wininger stresses the intentional use

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.658
of drugs, as a means to an end.

00:03:29.658 --> 00:03:32.730
In the psychedelic community
there's an emphasis

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.836
on integrating psychedelic experiences,

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:39.526
that is learning to
turn states into traits,

00:03:39.526 --> 00:03:42.580
learning to turn the
experience of, for example

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:45.800
a good MDMA high to translate that

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.800
into your life and bring it
into your life in a sober way

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.950
because we spend most of
our times, hopefully, sober.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.530
It's taken a long time

00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:00.190
for the culture to largely
forget the polarization

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.007
and violent polarization that happened

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:06.670
in the sixties and early seventies and

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.530
and have that generation
gap reduced and have traumas

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.770
like the Vietnam war become a memory

00:04:13.311 --> 00:04:16.891
so that psychedelics can now re-emerge

00:04:16.891 --> 00:04:21.414
in using the medical
model as a healing force

00:04:21.414 --> 00:04:25.772
in a time when we need healing very much.

00:04:25.772 --> 00:04:29.392
As psychedelics become more
mainstream and people realize

00:04:29.392 --> 00:04:33.252
that they can use it to
restore the lost part

00:04:33.252 --> 00:04:34.332
of their relationship.

00:04:34.332 --> 00:04:37.802
Or they can use it to build
upon a good relationship.

00:04:37.802 --> 00:04:41.212
They could use it to, we
call it recreational use.

00:04:41.212 --> 00:04:43.525
I prefer the term celebrational use

00:04:43.604 --> 00:04:46.941
Even as Wininger says that
MDMA and other substances

00:04:46.941 --> 00:04:49.825
can help shrink the gap
between generations,

00:04:49.825 --> 00:04:52.015
he also underscores that people's needs

00:04:52.015 --> 00:04:53.995
and bodies change with age

00:04:53.995 --> 00:04:56.425
Past the age of 35 or 40,

00:04:56.568 --> 00:04:59.195
the way to keep a happy,
long-term relationship

00:04:59.195 --> 00:05:03.392
with these wonderful
medicines is to not abuse them

00:05:03.392 --> 00:05:04.722
which takes some discipline

00:05:04.722 --> 00:05:07.380
because the experience can be so good

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.632
that you wanna do it again and again,

00:05:09.632 --> 00:05:13.521
but I limited to four five times a year.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.478
That way I could keep
it up into my seventies.

00:05:16.478 --> 00:05:18.406
So we plan these things very carefully

00:05:18.406 --> 00:05:21.968
and it gives us so much to
look forward to in life.

